<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dhi Qar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 609</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Dhi Qar was fought in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the early 7th century, probably between 604 and 611, between Arab tribes led by the Banu Shayban and a Sasanian Persian army with its Arab allies. The Arab victory followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>'s deposition of the Lakhmid king al-Nu'man III and was celebrated in later Arab tradition as the first triumph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. It exposed the weakness of the Sasanian desert frontier before the Muslim conquests.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 6th century the Lakhmid kingdom, a client of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, projected Persian power into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and shielded it from nomadic raids. Seeking direct control, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> deposed the Lakhmids in 602. King al-Nu'man III travelled from the Lakhmid capital, al-Hira, to the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, where he was seized and sent to prison at Khanaqin; he died there of plague. The Persians made Iyas ibn Qabisa, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayy">Tayyi</a> tribe, governor of the region, which soon brought them into conflict with the Banu Bakr, a large tribal confederation of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and eastern Arabia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Persian sources do not mention the battle, and its story comes from two later Arabic versions. One goes back to Ibn al-Kalbi, who died in 819: the fall of the Lakhmids opened the way to Bakri raids, and when rival members of the Shayban kept raiding despite an agreement, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> imprisoned the Shaybani leader and demanded Bakri hostages, which were refused. The other, held to be more fanciful, goes back to Abu Ubayda, in which the Shaybani chief refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>'s demand to hand over the former king's family and armour.</p><p>The Bakris won despite poor coordination among their tribes. Traditional sources put the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and their Arab auxiliaries at 2,000 and 3,000, and the clash may have been no more than a skirmish. Muslim traditions date it anywhere from 602 to 624, a range modern scholars have narrowed to 604 to 611.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The battle, also called the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">Camel</a>'s Udder, is remembered as a turning point in Arab-Persian relations and a blow to Persian control over eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. It is sometimes seen as part of a longer Arab revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> that culminated in the early Muslim conquests a few decades later. Its classical account is in al-Tabari's History of the Prophets and Kings, and it appears earlier in the Khuzistan Chronicle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Dhu Qar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The loss of this buffer contributed to the Arab victory over Persian forces at <b>Dhi Qar</b> and left the frontier open to the armies of the Rashidun Caliphate, which took al-Hira in 633.</blockquote><a href="/state/lakhmid-kingdom">Lakhmid Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
